AWS IAM – Training Notes
Identity and Access Management
1. What is AWS IAM?
AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) is a service used to securely control access to AWS resources. It helps manage who can sign in to an AWS account and what actions they are allowed to perform.
•	IAM controls authentication and authorization.
•	IAM is used to manage users, groups, roles, and permissions.
•	IAM is available at no additional charge.
2. Authentication vs Authorization
 
Authentication means verifying who a user is. Authorization means deciding what that user is allowed to do.
Banking example:
•	Authentication: Logging in to a bank account using a username, password, or OTP.
•	Authorization: Checking whether the logged-in user is allowed to transfer money, view balance, or download a statement.
3. Main Components of IAM
 
IAM Users
An IAM user represents an individual person or application that needs access to AWS. A user can have console access, programmatic access, or both.
IAM Policies
An IAM policy is a JSON document that defines permissions. It specifies which actions are allowed or denied on which AWS resources.
Example policy structure:
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:ListAllMyBuckets",
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
IAM Groups
An IAM group is a collection of IAM users. Permissions can be assigned to a group, and users in that group receive those permissions.
IAM Roles
An IAM role provides temporary permissions that can be assumed by a trusted user, application, or AWS service. For example, an EC2 instance can use an IAM role to access an S3 bucket.
Quick comparison:
Component	Purpose
User	Represents an individual identity
Policy	Defines permissions in JSON
Group	Manages permissions for multiple users
Role	Provides temporary permissions to trusted entities
4. Practical: Create an IAM User
1.	Sign in to the AWS Management Console.
2.	Search for IAM and open the IAM service.
3.	Select Users from the left navigation panel.
4.	Choose Create user.
5.	Enter a user name, for example: rahul-developer.
6.	Select the required AWS Management Console access option.
7.	Complete the user creation steps.
5. Attach AmazonS3FullAccess Policy
By default, a newly created IAM user has no permissions to access AWS services.
8.	Open the IAM user you created.
9.	Go to the Permissions tab.
10.	Choose Add permissions.
11.	Select Attach policies directly.
12.	Search for AmazonS3FullAccess.
13.	Select the policy and choose Add permissions.
 
The AmazonS3FullAccess managed policy allows full access to Amazon S3. It is used here for a training demonstration.
6. Verify S3 Access
14.	Open a private or incognito browser window.
15.	Sign in using the IAM user's console sign-in details.
16.	Open the Amazon S3 service.
17.	Check whether the user can view S3 buckets and perform the actions allowed by the attached policy.
If access is denied, check whether the policy is attached correctly and whether any explicit deny or other access control is blocking the request.
7. Create an IAM Group
18.	Open the IAM service.
19.	Select User groups from the left navigation panel.
20.	Choose Create group.
21.	Enter a group name, for example: Developers.
22.	Select the IAM users to add to the group.
23.	Attach the required permissions policy to the group.
24.	Choose Create group.
Users in the group receive the permissions assigned to that group.
8. IAM Security Best Practices
•	Follow the principle of least privilege: grant only the permissions required.
•	Enable multi-factor authentication (MFA) for users.
•	Avoid using the root user for daily activities.
•	Use IAM roles for applications and AWS services where appropriate.
•	Review permissions regularly.
•	Do not share passwords or access keys.
9. Interview Questions
What is IAM?
A service used to manage identities and control access to AWS resources.
What is the difference between authentication and authorization?
Authentication verifies identity; authorization determines permitted actions.
What is an IAM policy?
A JSON document that defines permissions.
What is an IAM group?
A collection of IAM users managed together.
What is an IAM role?
An identity that provides temporary permissions to a trusted entity.
What happens when a new IAM user is created?
The user has no permissions by default unless permissions are assigned.
What is least privilege?
Giving only the minimum permissions required to perform a task.
